Question: How are dense retrieval and generative retrieval combined for finding relevant documents?

# Combining Dense and Generative Retrieval

The Hierarchical Corpus Encoder (HCE) integrates dense retrieval and generative retrieval through a hybrid architecture that leverages the strengths of both approaches (chen2025hierarchicalcorpusencoder pages 1-2).

During training, HCE employs contrastive learning with hierarchical negative samples derived from sibling nodes in a document hierarchy, mimicking the loss function characteristic of generative retrieval methods (chen2025hierarchicalcorpusencoder pages 1-2). This training strategy enables the model to learn discriminative document representations while incorporating structural information from the corpus hierarchy.

At inference time, HCE performs retrieval using Maximum Inner Product Search (MIPS) with an external index, operating identically to traditional dense encoders (chen2025hierarchicalcorpusencoder pages 1-2). This design choice preserves the computational efficiency and scalability advantages of dense retrieval systems.

This hybrid approach achieves superior performance compared to either dense or generative methods in isolation (chen2025hierarchicalcorpusencoder pages 1-2). Additionally, the architecture enables efficient document addition and removal without requiring model fine-tuning, and supports zero-shot adaptation to new domains (chen2025hierarchicalcorpusencoder pages 1-2). The combination thus balances the training-time benefits of generative retrieval with the inference-time efficiency of dense retrieval.

